{"name":"Ocean Trapeze","hex":"#2e526a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ocean","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the large bodies of water separating the continents.","The continuous body of salt water covering a majority of the Earth's surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ocean"},{"term":"trapeze","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A swinging horizontal bar suspended at each end by a rope, used by circus artists.","A trapezium."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To swing on or as on a trapeze."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/trapeze"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ocean","blocks":[{"text":"The ocean is the body of salt water that covers approximately 70.8% of Earth. The ocean is conventionally divided into large bodies of water, which are also referred to as oceans, and are themselves mostly divided into seas, gulfs, and subsequent bodies of water. The ocean contains 97% of Earth's water and is the primary component of Earth's hydrosphere, acting as a huge reservoir of heat for Earth's energy budget, as well as for its carbon cycle and water cycle, forming the basis for climate and weather patterns worldwide. The ocean is essential to life on Earth, harboring most of Earth's animals and protist life. It originated photosynthesis and therefore Earth's atmospheric oxygen, and still supplies half of it."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg/330px-Pacific_Ocean_as_viewed_from_GOES-18_on_September_23%2C_2023.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ocean","wikidata":"Q9430"},{"title":"Trapeze","blocks":[{"text":"A trapeze is a short horizontal bar hung by ropes, metal straps, or chains, from a ceiling support. It is an aerial apparatus commonly found in circus performances. Trapeze acts may be static, spinning, swinging or flying, and may be performed solo, double, triple or as a group act."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Trapeze_Artists_in_Circus.jpg/330px-Trapeze_Artists_in_Circus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trapeze","wikidata":"Q1200918"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif/500px-Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif/500px-Mid-ocean_ridge_system.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":" Bathymetry of the ocean floor showing the continental shelves and oceanic plateaus (red), the mid-ocean ridges (yellow-green) and the abyssal plains (blue to purple)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMid-ocean_ridge_system.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/NJ_LBI_Lighthouse_04.JPG/500px-NJ_LBI_Lighthouse_04.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/NJ_LBI_Lighthouse_04.JPG/500px-NJ_LBI_Lighthouse_04.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Lighthouse at the coast of Ocean County, New Jersey, U.S., facing the Atlantic Ocean at sunrise","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANJ_LBI_Lighthouse_04.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Season-long_composites_of_ocean_chlorophyll_%288161799575%29.jpg/500px-Season-long_composites_of_ocean_chlorophyll_%288161799575%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ad/Season-long_composites_of_ocean_chlorophyll_%288161799575%29.jpg/1280px-Season-long_composites_of_o